# Kodo — Architecture Rules

Kodo is an Architecture Control Center for multi-stack codebases (Spring Boot, C#/.NET, Node.js).
This file is binding for every human and AI contributor.

## 1. Core IR is the single source of truth

- The canonical contract lives in `packages/core-ir/schema.json` (JSON Schema 2020-12).
- The same model MUST be implemented identically in three languages:
  - Python: `services/api-python/app/models/ir.py` (Pydantic v2)
  - C#: `services/analyzer-csharp/Models/CoreIr.cs` (records)
  - TypeScript: `apps/web/src/types/core-ir.ts`
- Any change to `WorkspaceIR`, `ComponentNode`, `ComponentRelation` requires updating the schema **and** all three implementations in the same change.
- `WorkspaceIR` is assembled by the orchestrator (`mermaidClassDiagram`, `warnings`, `audit`); `services/analyzer-csharp` only implements `ComponentNode`, `ComponentRelation` and its own request/response envelopes.
- Do not add fields to one implementation only. Do not rename wire fields. Do not invent parallel DTOs that drift from the contract.

## 2. Layer boundaries (no cross-layer leaks)

```
apps/web  ──HTTP/JSON──▶  services/api-python  ──HTTP/JSON──▶  services/analyzer-csharp
(UI only)                 (orchestrator, IR owner)             (Roslyn, C# AST only)
```

- `apps/web` MUST NOT parse source code, infer architecture, or know about Roslyn/Tree-sitter internals.
  It renders the Core IR and reads file text through `GET /api/v1/file`.
- `services/api-python` is the only orchestrator. It owns scanning, IR assembly, relation resolution,
  Mermaid generation, warning aggregation, the `/api/v1/dry-run` patch plan, and — for Phase 3c — the
  guarded `/api/v1/apply` + `/api/v1/apply/rollback` write path. It calls the C# analyzer's `/patch`
  endpoint and never returns that text to disk without the apply pre-flight.
- `services/analyzer-csharp` is a pure, stateless AST service. It knows nothing about the frontend,
  Mermaid, or orchestration. It never reads or writes workspace files; it receives file text and
  returns Core IR or proposed text.
- No shared code between services via file imports. Only the JSON contract crosses process boundaries.

## 3. Write policy (hard rule)

Phases 1 (visualizer), 2 (pattern audit), 3a (draft editing) and 3b (dry-run AST patching) are
**100% zero-write** with respect to user source code. Phase 3c adds exactly one, tightly guarded
write path (see §3c). Anything else stays read-only.

Forbidden:
- Writing, patching, moving, or deleting user source files outside the guarded apply service.
- Automatic apply / "fix it for me" disk mutations without an explicit user confirmation.
- Mutating Git state on the user's repository (add, commit, checkout, reset, stash, tag, clean).
- LLM/DeepSeek clients of any kind.
- Dockerfile / compose generation for the scanned workspace.

Allowed:
- Reading source text from a root that the user explicitly provides.
- Parsing it in memory and returning Core IR.
- Running the deterministic audit engine over the Core IR and returning `WorkspaceIR.audit`.
- Applying AST rewrite operations to **in-memory** syntax trees/text (Tree-sitter byte offsets for
  Java/TS/JS, Roslyn syntax rewriting for C#) and returning `originalContent` / `proposedContent`
  through `POST /api/v1/dry-run`. The returned text is never written by the dry-run itself.
- Inspecting Git status read-only through `app/services/git_safety.py` (`GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`, no
  mutating subcommands).
- Writing reviewed patches through `app/services/apply_service.py` only (§3c).
- Serving cached in-memory analysis results and rollback snapshots to the UI.

## 3a. Audit engine rules

- Every audit rule is deterministic, pure and derives from `WorkspaceIR` nodes/relations only.
  No filesystem access, no network, no clock, no randomness.
- Rules are registered through `app.audit.engine.audit_rule` and live in `app/audit/rules/`.
  Adding a rule must not require touching the orchestrator.
- Finding ids are stable (`RULE_ID:target`) and findings are emitted in a deterministic order
  (severity, ruleId, id) so two scans of the same tree produce byte-identical reports.
- The score is a fixed penalty per finding severity (error 10, warning 4, info 1), clamped to 0–100.
- Findings name the exact components involved and always include a concrete, human-applied suggestion.

## 3b. Dry-run patching rules

- The browser's only patching entry point is `POST /api/v1/dry-run` on the orchestrator. The UI must
  not call Roslyn directly or know Tree-sitter/Roslyn internals.
- `services/analyzer-csharp` exposes `POST /patch` for the orchestrator only. It is stateless:
  `{ filePath, content, operations }` in, `{ proposedContent, appliedOps, warnings }` out. No
  filesystem access, no writes.
- Java and TS/JS patchers must make surgical byte-offset edits inside the target type body and
  preserve every untouched token, method, comment and line ending. Regenerating whole files is
  forbidden.
- The C# patcher must use `CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText` + trivia-preserving rewriting. Methods,
  comments and formatting outside the targeted member/attribute stay verbatim.
- Every patcher degrades gracefully: unsupported constructs produce a warning and leave the file's
  proposed content byte-identical; they never raise a 500 and never write.
- Patching is bounded like inspection: strict path containment, 2 MB per file, and a bounded number
  of operations per request.

## 3c. Safe-write rules (apply to disk)

- **Every disk write goes through `services/api-python/app/services/apply_service.py`.** No route,
  service, analyzer or patcher may call `open(..., "w")`, `Path.write_text`, `Path.write_bytes`,
  `shutil` or `os.replace` on workspace files. The only writer is `_atomic_write()`.
- Entry point is `POST /api/v1/apply` (and `POST /api/v1/apply/rollback`). Both are synchronous
  FastAPI handlers so blocking Git/file I/O runs on the threadpool, and both take a module lock so
  applies and rollbacks never interleave.
- Pre-flight happens **before any byte is written**, in this order:
  1. Resolve each `filePath` and reject absolute paths, `..`, symlink escapes, non-files and files
     over the 2 MB cap (`PATH_REJECTED`, `FILE_TOO_LARGE`, `NOT_UTF8`).
  2. Inspect Git status read-only. Dirty tree without `allowDirty` → HTTP 409 `GIT_DIRTY` (with
     `dirtyFiles`). Not a repo / no `git` binary without `allowNonGit` → HTTP 409 `NOT_A_GIT_REPO`.
  3. Verify every file's current SHA-256 against the `expectedSha256` the dry-run returned. Any
     mismatch aborts the **entire** batch with HTTP 409 `STALE_FILE` (with `paths`). Partial applies
     are forbidden.
- Dry-run responses must include `originalSha256` (SHA-256 of the exact bytes read from disk), and the
  UI must echo it back untouched. Never re-hash a stale string client-side.
- Writes are atomic: sibling temp file → `fsync` → `os.replace`, bytes written as UTF-8 **without**
  newline translation (the patchers preserve the file's own CRLF/LF style). A failure mid-batch
  restores every file already written in that batch.
- Keep an in-memory rollback snapshot (`applyId`, `rootPath`, timestamp, `{filePath, previousContent,
  writtenSha256}`) bounded to the last 10 batches. `apply/rollback` verifies the files still match
  `writtenSha256` before restoring and aborts the whole rollback on a mismatch unless `force: true`.
  Batches are single-use and lost on restart; the UI must say so before a non-Git apply.
- Whenever a file is applied or rolled back, the UI clears the draft and re-runs `POST /api/v1/scan`
  so the graph, UML and health score reflect disk state immediately.
- Never add "apply on save", auto-apply or watch-mode writes. A human must press the Apply button and
  acknowledge any dirty/non-Git override explicitly.

## 4. Filesystem safety

- Every path coming from the client is untrusted. Resolve it, then verify it is inside the requested root
  (`Path.resolve()` + `is_relative_to`) before opening. Reject traversal with HTTP 400.
- Never follow symlinks out of the root. Never expose absolute filesystem paths to the UI except the
  scanned `rootPath` the user supplied.
- Skip `node_modules`, `.git`, `bin`, `obj`, `target`, `dist`, `.venv` when walking.
- Enforce file count / file size limits and report them as `WorkspaceIR.warnings`, not exceptions.
- Dry-run patching and apply repeat the containment check per file, reject absolute paths and `..`,
  and enforce the 2 MB cap. Apply additionally re-checks containment on every rollback target.

## 5. Degradation, never crashing

- The C# analyzer is optional at runtime. If it is offline, the orchestrator MUST still return
  Java/TS/JS results and append a human-readable entry to `WorkspaceIR.warnings`.
- Unparsable files, unknown extensions, and oversized files produce warnings, never 500s.
- The UI must render a usable empty state when `nodes` is empty.

## 6. Code style

- Strong typing everywhere: Pydantic v2 models (no raw dicts in the IR path), C# records + nullable
  annotations, TypeScript `strict` with no `any` in the IR path.
- Keep analyzers pure: `parse(text, context) -> nodes + references`. No I/O inside parsing functions.
- Deterministic output: stable node ordering (stack, filePath, name) and deduplicated ids/relations so
  scans of the same tree are diffable.
- Files under ~400 lines. One responsibility per module.
- Comments only where the code cannot explain itself.

## 7. Definition of done for any change

- `dotnet build services/analyzer-csharp` succeeds.
- `python -m pytest services/api-python/tests` succeeds.
- `python -m ruff check` on changed Python files succeeds.
- `npm run build` in `apps/web` succeeds (type-check + bundle).
- The full flow works end to end: scan a folder -> graph nodes appear -> UML renders -> file opens
  read-only.
- For patching changes: `docker compose up -d --build analyzer-csharp`, verify `GET /health` and
  `POST /patch`, then scan -> edit a field -> **Preview Diff (Dry-Run)** and confirm the proposed
  content preserves hand-written methods/comments.
- For apply changes: run the flow against a **git-initialised** temp project and confirm, in order,
  dry-run -> apply changes the file on disk and preserves business methods, the re-scan reflects the
  new health score, a dirty tree or a non-Git folder is rejected with 409 until overridden, an
  externally modified file yields 409 `STALE_FILE` with **no** file written, and rollback restores the
  original bytes exactly (`git status` clean again).
